  The attackers had the 158th Dragons in reserve.  Tarsus led his armies 
boldly into the battle.  No generals were present to lead the forces of the 
provincial Eagle chieftain.
  Luckily for Tarsus, some bandits raided the provincial Eagle chieftain's 
rearguard, so Tarsus had the edge over his opponent.  As the fierce battle 
developed, the tactical abilities of the provincial Eagle chieftain and 
Tarsus seemed evenly matched and neither leader could gain a tactical 
advantage.
  Tarsus could make almost no use of the Dragons, who could not comprehend 
his military strategy.
  Across the entire front, the Crack 23rd Legion were arrayed against the 
Veteran 30th Eagles.  The 46th Dragons swooped down on the 30th Eagles and 
attacked without mercy.  The 30th Eagles soared over the 23rd Legion and 
attacked them.  On this battleground, the regimental band of the 23rd Legion 
was squashed by a boulder hurled from a catapult, which greatly deflated the 
spirits of the troops.  Even so, the 23rd Legion fought the 30th Eagles and 
was able to make some progress.
  Having found a vulnerable spot in the line of the host of the provincial 
Eagle chieftain, a final savage slashing attack finished off their enemy and 
won the day for Tarsus.
  Although Tarsus's forces were victorious, the provincial Eagle chieftain 
regrouped its men and prepared to contest Shasta.  The battle affected the 
quality of our forces:  the 25th Legion fought determinedly during the 
battle and came through with flying colors.  We can now call it an Elite 
unit.  The 46th Dragons was greatly inspired by the exciting battle.  We now 
designate it a Veteran unit.
  In late summer, Tarsus's attempted cast of the Tornado spell was 
unsuccessful.  A mysterious magical counterspell may have worked against his 
spell.

  In Shasta, Prometeo brought to bay the forces of the provincial Eagle 
chieftain.  Tarsus was in command of the attacking armies.  The defenders 
were led by the provincial Eagle chieftain.  The armies were drawn up into 
lines of battle as shown here:

